  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nThe next 7 days are expected to see a largely stagnant weather \npattern with upper level ridging over the western United States. \nThis weekend will feature much above average to extreme \ntemperatures in the high 90s to near 110 F. PWATs tonight will \nincrease to around 150% of normal providing just enough upper \nlevel moisture for widely scattered thunderstorm development. \nSoundings indicate deeply mixed inverted V soundings meaning \nstrong wind gusts >60 mph will be the main threat with these \nthunderstorms. The Storm Prediction Center has issued a risk level\n1 of 5 for severe storms for locations west of Rosebud County \nSaturday. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are expected Sunday \nwith strong wind gusts being the main hazard. Severe storms are\nnot expected. \n\nThe combination of much above average temperatures, minimum\nrelative humidity values in the teens, and thunderstorms producing\nincreased lightning density and strong winds will lead to \ncritical fire weather conditions Saturday. Sunday will see similar\nconditions to Saturday with lower thunderstorm potential bringing\nelevated fire weather conditions. \n\nThere is good ensemble agreement that Monday through Friday will \nfeature persistent upper level ridging. This leads to high\nconfidence in above average temperatures in the mid 90s to low\n100s F. Relative humidity values are generally expected to be in\nthe teens and low 20s %. With temperatures drying out fuels,\nelevated fire weather conditions are expected. The lack of strong\nwinds will be one factor helping to lower fire risk. Near to \nabove average PWAT values during this time are expected to bring \ndaily elevated moisture leading to low (10-20%) daily chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. The main hazards with any of these \nstorms will be strong wind gusts. \n\nFinally for those keeping up on the July 25th drama. Billings \nairport has never (since 1934) reached 100F on this date, but it \nreally should happen this year. Current forecast is for 102F, \nconsistent with 700mb temps of +16C and deep mixing. With the \nwater vapor transport and eventual convection on Saturday, it is \nat least plausible that cloud cover holds back surface heating \njust a bit. The July 25th record is 98F, so it is possible to \nbreak the record and still not reach 100F. Stay tuned.\n\nLT/JKL",
